# The `E9xxx` codes that are NOT an unbuilt form, and therefore owe no chapter marker (#74).
#
# `E9xxx` means *NotImplemented* — a form the language HAS and this compiler does not build —
# and `scripts/chapter-codes-check.sh` holds every code in the range to being named in a
# chapter, because a form nobody can use is a fact its chapter owes its reader.
#
# These are the exceptions, and they are exceptions for one reason: THERE IS NOTHING TO MARK.
# A wrong-arity call to `ok_or`, a field an `Err` does not have, `?` on an `int` — none of them
# is a form waiting to be built. They are programs the language rejects, and `errors.md`
# already says what `ok_or` takes; a `[not yet]` beside it would be a marker for a feature
# nobody is waiting for.
#
# THE RANGE IS WHAT IS REALLY IN QUESTION. #74 settled the same argument once already, for
# `fn main`'s PARAMETER: "a rejection and not a `NotImplemented`, because the language has one
# entry input and a different one is not a form waiting to be built" — which is `E3113`. The
# entry's RESULT is `E9096` and is on this list, one number away from the rule that was decided
# the other way. Moving these out of `E9xxx` spends twenty numbers this project retires rather
# than reuses, so the list is where they sit until that is decided.
#
# One `code<TAB>why` per line.
E9053	a carrier form over a type that is not a carrier — a type error, not an unbuilt form
E9061	`in` over elements with no `==` — a missing bound on the program's own type
E9062	`in` over a type the operator does not take — a type error
E9080	`?` on a type that is not a carrier — a type error
E9083	`!` on a type that is not a carrier — a type error
E9084	`??` on a type that is not a carrier — a type error
E9086	a method on an `Err` called with an argument it does not take — arity
E9087	a method an `Err` does not have — the `Error` interface is three names
E9088	`ok_or` called with no error — arity
E9089	`ok_or` called with more than one error — arity
E9090	`ok_or` called with something that is not an `Err` — a type error
E9091	`ok` called with an argument it does not take — arity
E9092	a method a carrier does not have
E9093	a member an enum type does not have — it answers `of(n)` and its variants
E9094	a method the receiver's type does not have
E9095	a pattern a `Result[T]` does not have — it has Left and Right
E9096	`fn main` declared with a result the entry does not have — see the header
E9100	a method a `map` does not have
E9101	a field an `Err` does not have — it has `msg` and `kind`
E9102	`..=` with no upper bound — a malformed range, not a form
